.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/parent-module Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.SH "parent-module [![Build Status](https://travis-ci.org/sindresorhus/parent-module.svg?branch=master)](https://travis-ci.org/sindresorhus/parent-module)"
.PP
.RS 4
Get the path of the parent module 
.RE
.PP

.PP
Node\&.js exposes \fRmodule\&.parent\fP, but it only gives you the first cached parent, which is not necessarily the actual parent\&.
.SH "Install"
.PP
.PP
.nf
$ npm install parent\-module
.fi
.PP
.SH "Usage"
.PP
.PP
.nf
// bar\&.js
const parentModule = require('parent\-module');

module\&.exports = () => {
    console\&.log(parentModule());
    //=> '/Users/sindresorhus/dev/unicorn/foo\&.js'
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
// foo\&.js
const bar = require('\&./bar');

bar();
.fi
.PP
.SH "API"
.PP
.SS "parentModule([filepath])"
By default, it will return the path of the immediate parent\&.
.SS "filepath"
Type: \fRstring\fP
.br
 Default: \fR\fR__filename\fP\fP

.PP
Filepath of the module of which to get the parent path\&.

.PP
Useful if you want it to work \fRmultiple module levels down\fP\&.
.SH "Tip"
.PP
Combine it with \fR\fRread-pkg-up\fP\fP to read the package\&.json of the parent module\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const path = require('path');
const readPkgUp = require('read\-pkg\-up');
const parentModule = require('parent\-module');

console\&.log(readPkgUp\&.sync({cwd: path\&.dirname(parentModule())})\&.pkg);
//=> {name: 'chalk', version: '1\&.0\&.0', …}
.fi
.PP
